test_page_store
===============

This module is a design experiment for compute/storage separation, not a
production page service.  It exposes a raw main-fork page from a standby
paused at an explicitly identified system, timeline, and replay position.
The position is checked again after the page copy.  An optional, bounded
history mode retains main-fork and visibility-map versions while storage
replay continues.

The TAP test illustrates why ordinary redo cannot always reconstruct a
live writer's evicted pages: heap command IDs and sequence state can differ
from the writer's runtime state.  This is expected recovery behavior, not
a correctness bug in physical replication.  full_page_writes is disabled
only to make the command-ID test exercise heap redo rather than an FPI.
The sequence test assumes the current SEQ_LOG_VALS value.

The second test connects an SMgr modifier to a physical batch endpoint via
libpq.  It redirects one relation on a second paused standby, removes its
local file from the expected path, and verifies real SQL, synchronous reads,
and AIO buffer completion.  It also tests a stalled request with an acquired
AIO handle, reconnect after error, and loss of the configured read view.
pg_buffercache eviction and a complete ordered result digest prevent a warm
cache or local-file fallback from satisfying the main test.

The third test uses retained versions.  Its compute stays at the baseline,
but storage replays a multi-page UPDATE, VACUUM truncation, reextension, and
DROP.  SQL on the older compute still returns its original result without a
local relation file.  An injection point stops capture after one page of a
WAL record: the old cut remains readable, the in-flight cut is unavailable.
Restart revokes the volatile history.  Capacity exhaustion stops collection
without changing older images or interrupting normal standby redo.

The fourth test has a compute that follows WAL.  It skips cold heap main-fork
redo, applies redo to cached pages, and fetches missing pages from retained
storage history.  Its local heap file is unavailable throughout UPDATE,
INSERT, pruning, truncation, and reextension.  Ordered result digests include
physical TIDs.  The test also exercises both orders of buffer admission versus
redo, storage lag, and snapshot conflicts when the heap is entirely cold.

The fifth test retains two heaps and a B-tree index together.  It checks page
identity across relations, per-file truncation, and atomic publication of a
commit that drops all three files.  Old views remain readable while collection
is stopped between the first and second file's deletion metadata.

The sixth test has two following computes reading two remote heaps.  They
exercise opposite warm/cold redo paths and different replay positions.  A
lagging compute evicts its buffers and refetches older pages while the other
already sees a later transaction.  Extension, truncation, and reextension must
not mix up file sizes or pages belonging to different relations.

The seventh test enables a persistent history journal.  An older compute
continues fetching its pages after storage restarts, including a restartpoint
after DROP.  Crashes before a journal frame's footer and after fsync but before
publication preserve the previous cut.  A complete frame with a bad checksum
is rejected, not mistaken for an incomplete append.

The eighth test follows both a heap and its B-tree without local main-fork
files.  It exercises warm/cold insertion, leaf and internal splits, a new
root, page deletion, and page recycling.  Concurrent forward and backward
scans cross split boundaries; a separate schedule reads a page after redo
has skipped it.  Ordered value/TID digests use a sequential-scan oracle,
and amcheck with heapallindexed supplements those comparisons.

The ninth test retains VM history through setting and clearing bits, implicit
fork creation, truncation, reextension, DROP, and storage restart.  Truncation
can clear the last VM page without changing the file size or registering a
WAL block reference.  Disable hint FPIs in this test so they cannot hide a
missing capture of that change.

The tenth test follows heap, B-tree, and VM together.  Real index-only scans
run without their local files; results must match the writer's sequential
scan.  It covers both VM admission orders, implicit creation, warm/cold
clearing, truncation, reextension, and aborted insertion.  When all pages
are visible, EXPLAIN ANALYZE must report no heap fetches.

The eleventh test starts retention before taking the compute seed.  It removes
the selected heap, B-tree and VM files before first startup.  Compute then
bootstraps metadata at its recovery start, crashes before and after a new
restartpoint, and restarts alongside storage.  Ordered values and physical
TIDs, a transaction spanning the restartpoint, amcheck, and a real index-only
scan check that neither local files nor the provider's latest state substitute
for the required historical view.

The twelfth test saves a new seed from an already following compute using
ordinary pg_basebackup.  Its selected main, VM and FSM files are absent, including
any renamed copies inside PGDATA.  After losing that compute's entire PGDATA,
a replacement materializes the seed and fetches both WAL and pages from
storage.  It checks transaction and commit-timestamp status, multixact members,
prepared outcomes, catalog mappings, and replication origin progress.  A
dedicated storage slot protects the seed's WAL while restartpoints advance.

The thirteenth test exercises the physical wire protocol independently of the
libpq consumer: binary batches and metadata, request identity, malformed input,
query cancellation, CopyDone, and shutdown with an idle connection.  It drops
the SQL extension and denies database connections through pg_hba.conf while
continuing to fetch retained pages.  This raw fixture uses private Unix sockets;
the libpq consumer is exercised by 011 and 012 on all platforms.

The fourteenth test uses one shared transport slot and two backends.  It
checks backpressure, one physical connection for both callers, timeout with
an acquired AIO handle, reuse of the timed-out slot before the reply arrives,
and worker restart with a request outstanding.  The selected local file is
absent.  Distinct page images catch misrouted replies, and a full value/TID
digest checks that the backend remains usable after failed I/O.

The fifteenth test removes main, VM and FSM files before compute startup.
Warm heap prune redo rebuilds free-space hints, which can be lost on eviction
without a storage request.  Truncation, reextension, standalone FSM full-page
images and compute restart must preserve exact SQL results and amcheck output
without recreating those files.

Run with an assertions/injection-points build:

    make -C src/test/modules/test_page_store check

The tests require pageinspect, pg_buffercache, pg_visibility, pg_freespacemap,
amcheck, and
test_aio.  Injection
point schedules are skipped when injection_points is unavailable.  Autotools
EXTRA_INSTALL supplies these dependencies; Meson test lists include them.

For a manual disposable experiment, use two standbys recovered from the
same backup to the same named restore point, with recovery_target_action
set to pause.  On the storage node, create this extension on its upstream
primary before taking the backup.  On the compute node, configure:

    shared_preload_libraries = 'test_page_store'
    smgr_chain = 'test_page_store, md'
    test_page_store.conninfo = 'host=... port=... dbname=postgres user=...'
    test_page_store.replay_lsn = '...'
    test_page_store.replay_tli = 1
    test_page_store.tablespace = 1663
    test_page_store.database = ...
    test_page_store.relfilenumber = ...

Use the physical locator, not the table OID, and the storage's *replayed*
LSN after reaching the target, not the restore-point function's return
value.  The test script is the exact reproducible setup.  Do not remove
files from a real cluster: moving the file is only a test oracle.

Retained-history experiment
---------------------------

On storage, preload test_page_store and set test_page_store.history_pages to
the maximum number of retained page images (for example, 2048).  Start an
ordinary streaming standby, catch up, and administratively pause replay with
pg_wal_replay_pause().  While paused, call:

    SELECT test_page_store_retain('the_table');

To retain several relations at the same baseline, use one call instead:

    SELECT test_page_store_retain_relations(
        ARRAY['the_table', 'another_table', 'the_table_pkey']::regclass[]);

The immutable registry accepts at most 16 physical relations.  Images include
the registry entry, fork, and block number; sizes and existence are per file.
Main and VM forks share each published cut, including an absent VM that is
later created by heap redo without a separate SMGR_CREATE record.  FSM is
unlogged heuristic state and is not retained by this mechanism.

The returned LSN is the baseline view.  Resume storage replay normally; a
compute frozen at that baseline can keep using it through the same physical
fetch endpoint.  Recovery-target pause is unsuitable for the storage node:
resuming that pause finishes recovery instead of continuing as a standby.

test_page_store_history_status() reports the retained bounds, image/record
counts, any reason collection stopped, and the startup process's in-flight
LSN.  The latter is diagnostic, NOT a published read view.  Requests must use
an exact completed record end, not an arbitrary byte position in WAL.
The optional last argument of test_page_store_fetch(), wait_for_replay,
waits up to five seconds for publication of that record.  It does not make
an unavailable old cut available or permit a latest-page fallback.

Pages are copied after ordinary redo and consistency checking, before the
record is published as replayed.  File lifecycle changes are separate from
page images.  In particular, a dropped relation is absent even if md retains
an empty segment until checkpoint, and reextension must not expose an older
image of the same block number.  Unknown special relation updates stop
collection.  This prototype does not reproduce resource-manager redo.

The journal is deliberately simple: a fixed set of physical main and VM forks,
append-only shared-memory images, at most 65536 record boundaries, and no
garbage collection.  Reads search the image history linearly.  Do not use it
for performance claims.  Only startup appends after baseline publication;
unpublished slots are inaccessible to readers.  By default, restart clears the history,
and reinitialization cannot overwrite a live history.  This is fail-closed
view loss, NOT durable historical recovery or a view-lease implementation.

Optional persistent journal
---------------------------

Set test_page_store.history_durable = true and fsync = on on storage before
starting it.  The same retain call then writes the baseline through a temporary
file and durable rename.  Each complete WAL record appends a checksummed frame
with its new images and all selected files' metadata to PGDATA/test_page_store.history.
The frame is synced before its cut becomes visible to page requests.

At restart, startup verifies and reloads the journal before collecting more
history.  Replaying WAL already covered by the journal leaves its images
unchanged.  A short final frame is removed; a checksum failure in a complete
frame, wrong system identity, or incompatible local format is an error.  A
restartpoint beyond a missing journal interval cannot silently continue the
history.  This is tested with process crashes, not emulated power loss.

This remains a bounded scaffold.  It reloads all retained images into shared
memory, uses a native local format tied to this build, and syncs once per record.
Format version 2 adds fork identity and metadata; version 1 is not accepted.
Keep history_pages large enough for the existing journal after restart.  There
is no compaction, view GC, timeline transition, or backup protocol for the
journal.  Its fsync cost and linear image searches are not representative of
a mature provider.  Storage page history alone cannot recover local catalogs
or other nonselected state; replacement also needs a metadata seed.

Physical page-service session
-----------------------------

Set test_page_store.physical_service = true on compute to use a physical
replication connection instead of SQL.  libpq handles the normal PostgreSQL
connection, authentication and socket protocol.  The conninfo's replication
setting is overridden with replication=true; no database is opened.  Storage
must preload test_page_store and retain history as described above.  SQL
objects are only needed for the current administrative retention setup, not
for serving already retained pages.

The private TEST_PAGE_SERVICE command enters CopyBoth.  A greeting identifies
the prototype protocol version, PG_VERSION_NUM, BLCKSZ and system identifier.
Each binary request names its request ID, timeline and replay cut, plus the
physical locator, fork and block range for a page read.  The response echoes
that entire identity before returning metadata and bytes.  Integers use network
byte order; the raw pages still require physically compatible PostgreSQL builds.
Missing history and malformed responses cannot fall back to SQL or latest pages.
This is a draft test protocol, not a new public replication contract.

Requests are bounded at 64 bytes and batches at 64 pages.  A count of zero
returns fork existence and size; a separate predecessor request finds the
retained state before a recovery start.  CopyDone finishes the service command
and permits ordinary replication commands on the same connection.  A canceled
request produces an error; the transport discards that connection.  By default
there is one persistent connection per backend, with one request in flight.
Each connection consumes a max_wal_senders slot on storage.  The optional
shared transport below bounds that process fanout.  These sessions neither
stream nor acknowledge WAL and do not count as synchronous replication
acknowledgements.

The endpoint remains superuser-only.  Normal physical replication HBA rules
apply, and SSL can be handled by the existing connection layer; TLS is not
covered by this test.  Storage must reach ordinary hot standby readiness:
hot_standby=off also prevents incoming replication connections.  The service
does not bypass that startup safeguard.  It can, however, serve a compute
that has not yet opened SQL connections of its own.  There are no service
heartbeats or server-side idle deadlines yet.  test_page_store.request_timeout
sets the client deadline and can be changed by a superuser per session.

Shared transport worker
-----------------------

With physical_service enabled, set test_page_store.transport_slots to a value
from 1 to 64 on compute (default 0 disables the worker).  One early background
worker owns the libpq connection and serves a bounded FIFO queue.  It starts
before recovery consistency, without connecting to a database, so startup can
use it to bootstrap remote metadata.  Tests 011 and 012 use this path.

Each slot has an owner, generation, absolute deadline and fixed-size request
and response areas.  A caller waits for capacity, submits its request, then
copies a completed response into private memory before releasing the slot.
The worker never receives pointers into caller memory or shared buffers and
never owns the caller's AIO handle.  ERROR and process-exit cleanup release
the slot.  Timeout permits immediate reuse, but a worker may publish only
while the slot still names its running generation.  Late replies are discarded.
Worker exit marks outstanding requests failed; restart opens a new connection
on demand.  It does not silently retry failed requests.

test_page_store_transport_status() exposes queue occupancy and counters, the
worker PID and its wait event.  The worker has no database session and is not
in pg_stat_activity.  Its wait event permits deterministic test orchestration
without assuming a sleep duration or backend startup order.

This first worker is serial: it decouples connection ownership, not network
parallelism.  Callers still wait synchronously, preserving SMgr interrupt
holdoffs.  Query cancel therefore need not end a read before its transport
deadline, and startreadv is not yet truly asynchronous.  DNS can block the
worker outside its socket deadline, although callers can release their slots
on timeout.  Multiple in-flight requests, asynchronous completion and bounded
resolver/shutdown behavior remain separate work.  The extra response copies
and fixed slot sizes are also prototype tradeoffs, not performance claims.

Following read-only compute experiment
-------------------------------------

For a restartable compute, start storage history retention before taking the
compute seed backup.  Retained history must cover the seed checkpoint's REDO
point and every later compute restartpoint.  Configure the same locator,
timeline, and retained baseline as above, plus:

    test_page_store.follow = true

Do not set a recovery target.  Before the first redo, startup asks storage for
the last retained record end at or before its recovery start, then loads all
selected files' metadata at that cut.  A REDO point can follow WAL padding, so
it is not necessarily itself a retained record end.  Storage must have replayed
through the requested position before choosing this predecessor; an arbitrary
older or latest cut is not a substitute.

Shared buffers and per-page markers start empty; normal redo reconstructs the
markers without persistent local copies.  Missing history fails startup.  The
new test 011_compute_restart.pl includes the full setup and process crashes.
History collection and following compute must run on different nodes.

The older tests also support a seed whose recovery starts before retention.
That path uses local files until replay reaches the exact configured baseline.
It cannot bootstrap without those files and must not be used as the restartable
setup; moving files aside after activation was only an earlier test oracle.

For several heaps or B-tree indexes, replace tablespace/database/relfilenumber
with one list:

    test_page_store.locators = '1663/5/16384, 1663/5/16385'

Up to 16 physical locators may be selected, with separate file-size state and
LSN markers for each fork.  Main forks must exist at the retained baseline;
VM forks can first appear later.  The list cannot
be combined with the single-locator settings.  This remains temporary test
configuration, not a proposed user-facing placement policy.  The sixth test
shows this setup with two independent following computes.

The admission handshake uses the buffer mapping partition lock.  A mapped but
invalid buffer belongs to an admitted read, not an absent page.  Redo waits
for its I/O, or performs the read itself if it wins input I/O ownership, then
updates the buffer.  For an absent page, startup records the skipped record's
end LSN before releasing the mapping lock.  A subsequent read cannot use an older cut
and waits until compute has completed that record.  Eviction also preserves
the discarded page's LSN.  Publication of completed replay is after all rmgr
effects and consistency checks.  Storage can independently wait to catch up.

Following mode limits I/O to one page per request.  Owning input I/O for a
second page while waiting for a skipped record can deadlock with redo of that
second page.  The test reproduces this with a two-page UPDATE if the limit is
removed.  General asynchronous multi-page admission/completion is future work.

Only heap/B-tree main-fork and heap VM paths have been audited.  Init-page
redo and standalone full-page images always get a real buffer; this includes B-tree
metapages, new split/root pages, and half-dead/deleted page initialization.
B-tree split/unlink redo still retains its required sibling locks.  Read-only
descent and sibling walks release the previous content lock before reading
the next page.  VM reads retain pins but not content locks across heap reads.
Truncation's implicit VM update is not filtered and may fetch a cold page;
it retains the replay record's LSN so eviction preserves the cleared tail.
FSM is different: its contents are hints, not historical versions.  On a
following compute it exists only in shared buffers, with a volatile size.
Ordinary heuristic redo and full-page image replay still obtain real FSM
buffers.  Writes are discarded, and a later miss supplies zero pages locally,
which mean unknown free space.  It does not fetch FSM pages from storage or
wait for a replay cut.  Fresh compute startup starts with an empty FSM;
legacy local-seed activation retains the old size and any resident hints.
The storage node still has its ordinary, local FSM.  This is a read-only
compute policy, not permission to forget free-space state on a live writer
without considering its allocation behavior.

Other forks still receive ordinary local redo.  Snapshot
conflicts, including B-tree page-reuse conflicts, run even when page work is
skipped.  Prefetch is an advisory no-op, not a missing-file indication.
WAL consistency checking skips
only those compute blocks for which no local redo result exists; the storage
still performs and checks full redo.  test_page_store_compute_status() exposes
the completed position and skip/cache/fetch counters for test orchestration.

The per-page lower bounds are bounded dense arrays, with no eviction, sized by
test_page_store.follow_max_blocks (default 65536) per selected relation fork.
Unsupported rmgrs, a second
visit to the same selected block within a record, or exceeding this bound cause
an error.  Restart reconstructs the markers from WAL only when retained history
covers the recovery start as described above.  Promotion and replacement of
the selected relation are not supported.

Replacing a compute
-------------------

An existing following compute can supply a new metadata seed through
pg_basebackup.  The source must have full_page_writes enabled on its primary
and a replayed checkpoint with that setting.  The ordinary backup protocol
copies local catalogs, SLRUs, relmaps, two-phase state, control data, and the
required WAL; selected main/VM files that are absent are not copied.  Remove
obsolete selected FSM files as well: their disposable contents are not part
of the compute's recovery baseline.

Retain that seed outside the donor compute, alongside access to the matching
page history and WAL.  Before publishing it, check that storage retains WAL
from at least the backup manifest's Start-LSN.  A separate physical slot on
storage can enforce this in the prototype.  Creating a slot immediately
before backup is not sufficient if the donor is behind storage's restartpoint;
verify the boundary.  A live compute's advancing slot is not a seed lease.
For a replacement, materialize the saved metadata into a new PGDATA, configure
its own WAL receiver slot, and keep the page-service identity and history
settings.  See 012_compute_seed.pl for the complete disposable procedure.

This is NOT a self-contained backup of the database.  pg_verifybackup checks
the files and WAL in the seed, not its external page-history dependency.
Passing that check does not make it safe to discard storage.  A complete
provider/backup integration must represent these dependencies explicitly.
The first donor still uses an ordinary seed, and unselected relation files
are included in all subsequent seeds.  This does not cover all metadata
lifecycle paths or turn the existing writer into a remote-storage compute.

Important limitations:

* This is not diskless compute.  Unselected relations, catalogs, SLRUs, and
  other state remain local.  Following mode redirects only selected heap and
  B-tree main forks and VM; frozen mode leaves startup redo unchanged.
* Size-reporting functions still stat local files, bypassing SMgr.  In this
  experiment pg_relation_size() is not an oracle for a remote relation.
* Both compute modes are read-only.  Executor guards reject queries outside
  the configured mode, including cache-only queries after promotion.  Do
  not resume a frozen compute, run mutating utility operations, or treat
  these guards as a replacement for a storage lease.
* Hint writes for the selected relation are discarded.  Normal tuple or
  index writes cannot be treated this way on a live writer.
* Requests use a persistent, nonblocking libpq connection, either binary SQL
  results or the physical service, at most 64 pages per request, and one
  in-flight request.  Reads
  still wait synchronously in the calling backend with interrupts held.
  The deadline bounds socket waits, not external DNS resolution.  A worker
  transport with a safe cancellation lifetime remains to be implemented.
* General selective redo, remote WAL durability, and a writable compute are
  not implemented.
  Replacement from a published seed works for the selected-relation experiment,
  but seed publication and retention remain operator-managed.  No throughput
  claim is made.
* The original single-locator GUCs and timeline GUC use signed-int range;
  the locator list accepts unsigned OIDs.  Neither is a proposed wire format.

This module is discussion scaffolding, not a proposed public SQL API.
